  "summary": "An elongated communal dwelling serving as living quarters and ceremonial center for multiple families.",
  "description": "Features an elongated shape with steep pitched roofs and often elaborate carvings and paintings. Designed to accommodate extended families while maintaining separation between men's and women's spaces within the structure.",
  "culturalContext": "The Papuan and Melanesian longhouse — communal ceremonial architecture of Papua New Guinea's Highlands, Sepik and Asmat regions — houses clans under one sweeping thatch: carved spirit poles and gable masks (korogo, bai tambaran), central fire lanes, men's-house sanctums holding ancestors, and painted facade emblems of lineage. Cultures (Asmat, Chambri, Dani) vary the form; longhouse ceremonies remain the Pacific's great social architectures of performance and memory.",
